<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong Wei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/350">350</a>–385</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Emperor You of Former Yan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Wei was the last emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> as a child in 360. After the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> died, court jealousy drove the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> to Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. In 370 the Qin chancellor Wang Meng took Ye and captured Murong Wei, ending the state. He was later killed after a plot against Qin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murong Wei, courtesy name Jingmao, was born in 350, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> and Empress Kezuhun. He was made Prince of Zhongshan in 354 and crown prince in 357, after the death of his able elder brother Murong Ye, probably because he was the empress's son, though his father had older sons. At a feast in 359 the official Li Ji praised him but warned of his overindulgence in hunting and music, and Murong Wei bore him a grudge ever after. When Murong Jun fell ill in early 360, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> declined the throne and became regent for the boy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> was an able regent who made all major decisions, executed the plotting official Muyu Gen and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> from Jin in 365. The young emperor still sent Li Ji away into exile. When Murong Ke died in 367 he recommended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, but Empress Dowager Kezuhun and the jealous, corrupt Murong Ping kept power instead. Murong Ping refused to help the rebel dukes of Former Qin, though the reformer Yue Wan restored over 200,000 people to the tax rolls in 368. In 369 the Jin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> advanced to Fangtou near Yecheng before Murong Chui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-de">Murong De</a> defeated him.</p><p>The court then denied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> his rewards and considered killing him, so he fled to Fu Jiān of Former Qin, and it broke a promise to cede the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> region. Wang Meng invaded with 60,000 men, and in 370 crushed Murong Ping's army of 300,000, whose morale had collapsed after Murong Ping charged his own soldiers for firewood and water. Murong Wei fled Yecheng, was deserted by his guards and was captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fu Jiān made him Marquess of Xinxing and a general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and he served in the siege of Xiangyang in 378 and the campaign of 383. After the defeat at the Fei River his brothers Murong Hong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chong">Murong Chong</a> rebelled. Murong Wei wrote secretly to Murong Hong that he was a man in an iron cage, and with his cousin Murong Su planned to kill Fu Jiān at a feast for his son's wedding. The plot leaked, and Fu Jiān executed them and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> of the city. Southern Yan later named him Emperor You.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murong Wei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 370 the Qin chancellor Wang Meng took Ye and captured the emperor <b>Murong Wei</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.94&amp;year=372" aria-label="Xi'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7322 922 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Murong Wei"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7772 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7772 1192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.94&amp;year=372">What was here in 372 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
